<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.3.2: Using Completing the Square to Solve Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02369</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.3.2: Using Completing the Square to Solve Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>01fef4b0-22b6-4774-a366-8df757de2703</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">Completing the square</term> can be used to solve quadratic equations.</para>
<para>Example: Solve \(x^2+8x-2=0\).</para>
<para>To complete the square to solve an equation, follow these steps:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the equation in the form \(ax^2+bx=c\).
  \(x^2+8x=-2\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Complete the square by finding \((\frac{b}{2})^2\).
  \((\frac{b}{2})^2=(\frac{8}{2})^2=16\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Add the value to both sides of the equation.
  \(x^2+8x+16=-2+16\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Factor the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">perfect square</term> trinomial and simplify the constants.
  \((x+4)^2=14\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side (remember the plus-minus sign with the constant).
  \(\sqrt{(x+4)^2}=\sqrt{14}\)
  \(x+4= \pm \sqrt{14}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write two separate equations and solve each.
  \(x + 4 = \sqrt{14}\)  or  \(x + 4 =\; - \;\sqrt{14}\)
  \(x = -4 + \sqrt{14}\)  or  \(x =\; -\;4\; –\; \sqrt{14}\)</para>
<para>Solve each equation by completing the square. In part a, select the value of \((\frac{b}{2})^2\) that will complete the square. In part b, choose the <emphasis>two </emphasis>roots.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="008d3d4f-b998-4f27-83f7-aca8328cfb6b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter the value:</para><para>\((x−3)(x+1)=5\)</para>
      <para>What is the correct value of \((\frac{b}{2})^2\) that will complete the square?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \((\frac{b}{2})^2 = 1\)</para><para>The correct answer is \((\frac{b}{2})^2 = 1\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="79029430-6d86-490c-a7d2-3acf7a23fa03" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> roots that solve the quadratic equation.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x = -2\), \(x = 4\)</para><para>The correct answers are: \(x = -2\), \(x = 4\).</para></solution></exercise></section><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->

<!--Q#2a-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(x^2+\frac{1}{2}x=\frac{3}{16}\)</para>
    <para>What is the correct value of \((\frac{b}{2})^2\) that will complete the square?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
<para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((\frac{b}{2})^2= \frac{1}{16}\)</para>



</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->

<!--Interaction End -->

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="76c1f248-4edb-4a02-9d24-dab54110bcd7" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two </emphasis>roots that solve the quadratic equation.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=\frac14\), \(x=-\frac34\) </para><para>The correct answers are: \(x=\frac14\), \(x=-\frac34\).</para></solution></exercise></section><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->


<!--Q#3a-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(x^2+3x+\frac{8}{4}=0\)</para>
    <para>What is the correct value of \((\frac{b}{2})^2\) that will complete the square?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
<para>Compare your answer:</para>
   
   <para>\((\frac{b}{2})^2= \frac94\)</para>



</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->

<!--Interaction End -->

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="d20a0059-beb2-4112-8fb1-4ccc23cc7f14" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> roots that solve the quadratic equation.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x = -2\), \(x = -1\)</para><para>The correct answers are: \(x = -2\), \(x = -1\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="4bac0056-6a2a-4f4a-ae8f-753e654ff4ae" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>\((7−x)(3−x)+3=0\)</para>
      <para>What is the correct value of \((\frac{b}{2})^2\) that will complete the square?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \((\frac{b}{2})^2= 25\)</para><para>The correct answer is \((\frac{b}{2})^2= 25\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="abf2d852-e418-46c5-85cb-845a6773c2b7" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> roots that solve the quadratic equation.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x = 6\), \(x = 4\)</para><para>The correct answers are: \(x = 6\), \(x = 4\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="e8b33f01-81e0-4dfc-910c-6fc0f6987a44" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>\(x^2+1.6x+0.63=0\)</para>
      <para>What is the correct value of \((\frac{b}{2})^2\) that will complete the square?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \((\frac{b}{2})^2=0.64\)</para><para>The correct answer is \((\frac{b}{2})^2=0.64\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="c95204d9-28c9-4d83-a860-91c4b31263a6" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> roots that solve the quadratic equation.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x = -0.7\), \(x = -0.9\)</para><para>The correct answers are: \(x = -0.7\), \(x = -0.9\).</para></solution></exercise></section>

